{"qid":"Q4083686","names":["Sultanate of Bengal"],"title":"Sultanate of Bengal","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Bengal Sultanate was an independent Muslim state in the eastern Gangetic delta, its sovereignty emerging from the breakaway sultans of the 1330s and consolidated when Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah unified Gauda and Sonargaon by 1352 and won Delhi's recognition of Bengali independence in the wars of 1353-1359. Under the Ilyas Shahi and Ganesha dynasties and then the Hussain Shahi house from 1494, the sultanate extended from the Brahmaputra delta toward Bihar and Orissa, and its capital Gaur grew into one of the largest cities of the Islamic world, prized by traders as among the richest markets of the East. Through the early sixteenth century the sultanate remained the paramount delta power, campaigning as far as Assam against the Ahoms in 1532-33, on the eve of the upheavals that would bring Sher Shah Suri's Afghan conquest of Bengal in 1539.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Bengal Sultanate's core territory in the Ganges-Brahmaputra delta is well-attested through sultanate inscriptions and chronicles, but its northern and western boundaries fluctuated considerably across dynasties and precise border reconstructions are approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Bengal Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah","nativeName":"شمس الدین إلياس شاه","roman":"Shams al-Dīn Ilyās Shāh","start":1342,"end":1358,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Sikandar Shah I","nativeName":"سکندر شاه","roman":"Sikandar Shāh I